# Order flow, trade arrivals and adverse selection {#sec-orderflow}


::: {.content-visible when-profile="synthetic"}
::: {.callout-warning}
## Synthetic-data build
This page was rendered from **simulated** order books, not the licensed JSE data. Every number, table and figure was recomputed by the same code, but the written interpretation describes the real data and will not match it.
:::
:::


With a clean, signed and time-ordered event table, the stylised facts of order flow can be measured. These are the empirical regularities that any model of a limit order book must reproduce. Following @jericevich2020comparing, this chapter looks at three: the visual texture of the book over an hour, the persistence of the direction of trading, and the clustering of trade arrivals. An extension then asks whether larger trades pay more, as theories of adverse selection predict.

## The continuous session

Auction call periods, the uncross prints and the quote changes around them follow a different mechanism from continuous trading. They are removed before any statistic is computed [@jericevich2020comparing]: of 5\,206\,038 events, 5\,036\,231 (96\.7%) belong to the continuous session.

## An hour of the order book

Each bubble chart shows every change at the touch over an hour: bid updates in blue and ask updates in red, with area proportional to the size at the touch; trades as black circles with area proportional to volume; and the mid-price
$$
m_t = \frac{b_t + a_t}{2}
$$
and the micro-price
$$
\mu_t = \frac{b_t\, v^a_t + a_t\, v^b_t}{v^a_t + v^b_t},
$$
which leans towards the side with *less* resting volume, the side more likely to be depleted next. The charts are drawn from the merged, globally sorted file of @sec-pipeline. That file still records which side of the book changed at each event, which the forward-filled columns of the final dataset no longer show.

::: {#cell-fig-bubbles .cell execution_count=2}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Top of book and trades on 4 September 2013: a mid-morning hour and the last hour of the day (16:50–17:00 is the closing auction call).](03-order-flow_files/figure-html/fig-bubbles-output-1.png){#fig-bubbles width=1312 height=927}
:::
:::


The micro-price tracks the quotes closely and moves before the mid-price when one side of the book thins out. The last hour shows the change of regime at 16:50: continuous trading stops and the closing auction's call period begins. During the call, the book shows indicative and sometimes crossed quotes far from the market (cut off here) and a few very large orders arriving to be executed at the close.

### Depth behind the best price

::: {#cell-fig-depth .cell execution_count=3}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Ten-level order book at 10:30 SAST on 4 September 2013. Bars are positioned at the price level, with height equal to resting volume.](03-order-flow_files/figure-html/fig-depth-output-1.png){#fig-depth width=1312 height=432}
:::
:::


A depth snapshot shows how much liquidity sits behind the best price, and so how far a large order would move the price. At this moment the total bid volume over ten levels is 1\.56 times the ask volume for AGLJ.J and 1\.58 times for GRTJ.J. GRTJ.J's book is concentrated in fewer, much larger levels.

## Persistence of order flow

Let $\varepsilon_i \in \{-1, +1\}$ be the sign of the $i$-th trade. If order flow were unpredictable, the signs would be uncorrelated. Its sample autocorrelation
$$
\hat\rho(\ell) = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n-\ell}(\varepsilon_i - \bar\varepsilon)(\varepsilon_{i+\ell} - \bar\varepsilon)}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\varepsilon_i - \bar\varepsilon)^2}
$$
is computed by FFT in $O(n \log n)$ for $\ell$ up to 1,000. Under independence, $\hat\rho(\ell)$ is approximately $N(0, 1/n)$, which gives the band $\pm 1.96/\sqrt{n}$.

::: {#cell-fig-sign-acf .cell execution_count=4}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Autocorrelation of trade signs (continuous session), log-scaled lag axis.](03-order-flow_files/figure-html/fig-sign-acf-output-1.png){#fig-sign-acf width=1092 height=762}
:::
:::


Trade signs are strongly positively autocorrelated. The lag-1 autocorrelation is 0\.26 for AGLJ.J and 0\.31 for GRTJ.J, and it first falls inside the white-noise band at lag 153 and 151 respectively. Over the significant lags up to 100, a power law $\rho(\ell) \propto \ell^{-\gamma}$ fitted by least squares on the log–log scale gives $\hat\gamma =$ 0\.48 (AGLJ.J) and 0\.47 (GRTJ.J). Slow, power-law-like decay is the signature of large orders split into many small ones and executed over time [@lillo2003master; @toth2011anomalous]. It is also why order flow has to be modelled with memory, as in Part III.

## Clustering of trade arrivals

Let $\tau_i = t_{i+1} - t_i$ be the time between consecutive trades of the same security on the same day. If trades arrived as a Poisson process with constant rate $\lambda$, the $\tau_i$ would be independent $\text{Exp}(\lambda)$.

::: {#cell-fig-tau-hist .cell fig-height='7' execution_count=5}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Trade inter-arrival times: raw and log scale.](03-order-flow_files/figure-html/fig-tau-hist-output-1.png){#fig-tau-hist width=1312 height=762}
:::
:::


The mean waiting time is 11\.4 s for AGLJ.J and 24\.9 s for GRTJ.J. Many waiting times are essentially zero (0% and 0% within the same millisecond): one aggressive order prints at several prices at once. On a log scale the distribution has two modes: bursts of activity within a second, and quiet spells of tens of seconds to minutes.

::: {#cell-fig-tau-qq .cell execution_count=6}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Quantiles of inter-arrival times against an exponential distribution with the same mean.](03-order-flow_files/figure-html/fig-tau-qq-output-1.png){#fig-tau-qq width=1202 height=542}
:::
:::


Both QQ-plots bend above the line in the upper tail: long waits are far longer than an exponential with the same mean allows. This is evidence against a memoryless Poisson process.

### A power-law tail

For the tail, a continuous power law $p(x) = \frac{\alpha - 1}{x_{\min}}\left(\frac{x}{x_{\min}}\right)^{-\alpha}$ for $x \ge x_{\min}$ is fitted by maximum likelihood [@clauset2009power]:
$$
\hat\alpha = 1 + n\left[\sum_{i:\, x_i \ge x_{\min}} \ln\frac{x_i}{x_{\min}}\right]^{-1},
$$
with $x_{\min}$ chosen to minimise the Kolmogorov–Smirnov distance between the empirical and fitted tail distributions. Starting the fit at the smallest observation instead would mix the burst regime into the tail estimate and bias $\hat\alpha$ towards 1.

::: {#cell-fig-tau-pl .cell execution_count=7}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Complementary CDF of inter-arrival times (log–log) with the maximum-likelihood power-law tail.](03-order-flow_files/figure-html/fig-tau-pl-output-1.png){#fig-tau-pl width=1312 height=487}
:::
:::



```python
# jsemicro.eda.order_flow.fit_power_law
def fit_power_law(x, xmin_method="clauset", n_candidates=200, min_tail=50):
    """Power-law tail fit. With ``xmin_method="clauset"`` the lower cut-off is
    the candidate that minimises the Kolmogorov-Smirnov distance between the
    empirical and fitted tail CDFs; ``"min"`` uses the smallest positive value."""
    x = np.sort(np.asarray(x, float))
    x = x[x > 0]
    if xmin_method == "min":
        a, n = power_law_mle(x, x[0])
        return {"alpha": a, "xmin": x[0], "n_tail": n, "ks": np.nan}
    cands = np.unique(np.quantile(x, np.linspace(0, 0.99, n_candidates)))
    best = None
    for xm in cands:
        t = x[x >= xm]
        if len(t) < min_tail:
            break
        a = 1.0 + len(t) / np.sum(np.log(t / xm))
        emp = np.arange(len(t)) / len(t)
        fit = 1.0 - (t / xm) ** (1.0 - a)
        ks = np.max(np.abs(emp - fit))
        if best is None or ks < best["ks"]:
            best = {"alpha": a, "xmin": xm, "n_tail": len(t), "ks": ks}
    return best
```



The fitted tail exponents are $\hat\alpha =$ 2\.96 for AGLJ.J ($x_{\min} =$ 49 s, 3\,029 waits in the tail) and 3\.82 for GRTJ.J ($x_{\min} =$ 290 s). Starting at the smallest positive wait gives the misleading 1\.15 and 1\.14. A tail exponent below 3 would mean that a pure power law has infinite variance.

### Memory in waiting times

::: {#cell-fig-tau-acf .cell execution_count=9}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Autocorrelation of inter-arrival times.](03-order-flow_files/figure-html/fig-tau-acf-output-1.png){#fig-tau-acf width=1092 height=652}
:::
:::


Waiting times are positively autocorrelated: 100 of the first 100 lags exceed the 95% band for AGLJ.J, and 100 for GRTJ.J. Short waits follow short waits and long waits follow long waits, so trading intensity itself clusters in time. A heavy-tailed marginal distribution together with positive serial dependence is what a self-exciting point process produces. Part III fits such a process.

## Extension: do larger trades pay more?

If some traders are better informed, liquidity providers lose to them on average and protect themselves by charging more to trades that are more likely to be informed. Large trades are more likely to be informed [@easley1987price]. Two measures, both oriented as a cost to the trader who initiates the trade, are tracked by trade size. With $\varepsilon_i$ the trade sign, $p_i$ the trade price, $m_i$ the prevailing mid-price and $\Delta m_i$ the change in the mid-price across the trade:
$$
\text{slippage}_i = \varepsilon_i\,(p_i - m_i), \qquad \text{impact}_i = \varepsilon_i\, \Delta m_i .
$$
Slippage is the immediate cost of execution relative to fair value: half the spread for a small trade, more for an order that walks the book. Impact is the revision of the fair value itself, which reflects the information the market infers from the trade. Trades are grouped into 20 logarithmically spaced bins of normalised volume $\tilde v \in [10^{-3}, 10]$, as in @jericevich2020comparing, separately for buys and sells.

::: {#cell-fig-adverse .cell fig-height='8' execution_count=10}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Average slippage (left, ZAc) and signed price impact (right, log mid-price change) by normalised-volume bucket.](03-order-flow_files/figure-html/fig-adverse-output-1.png){#fig-adverse width=1312 height=872}
:::
:::


::: {#cell-fig-vwap-bucket .cell execution_count=11}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![AGLJ.J: volume-weighted average execution price against the average pre-trade mid-price, by size bucket.](03-order-flow_files/figure-html/fig-vwap-bucket-output-1.png){#fig-vwap-bucket width=1311 height=487}
:::
:::


For small trades the slippage is roughly half the spread: on average 7\.1 c for AGLJ.J and 1\.3 c for GRTJ.J for trades below a tenth of the daily average size. A regression of the bucket averages on $\log_{10}\tilde v$, weighted by the number of trades, gives the change per tenfold increase in size. Slippage changes by \-0\.47 c (AGLJ.J) and \-0\.02 c (GRTJ.J), and the price impact, a log change, by \+0\.21 and \+0\.29 basis points. At the level of individual prints, then, there is little sign that larger trades pay more. Slippage does not rise with size: large prints mostly execute at or inside the touch. Impact rises only slightly. This does not mean there is no adverse selection. The decomposition of @glosten1988estimating separates the spread into an order-processing component and an adverse-selection component, the permanent impact. Part II measures that decomposition properly, with the mid-price a minute after each trade rather than at the next event (@sec-costs). Part II also finds that single-trade impact barely depends on size (@sec-impact): large traders split their orders, so the size of a single print says little about the information behind it.
